s the whole biochemical description defining the disease, not just the disease name itself — so the discrepancy here is really about span boundaries the model missed rather than entity-type disagreement.\n\n\"Deficiency\" functions as a state noun, so the distinguishing factor seems to be whether that deficiency is itself the disease-defining pathology (like G6PD deficiency) versus just a mechanistic descriptor within a sentence about an already-named disease. Comparing against true positives, those tend to be lexicalized disease names ending in canonical disease-head nouns like \"disease,\" \"syndrome,\" or \"cancer.\"\n\nInterestingly, within the same document, the model correctly tags \"G6PD deficiency\" as a premodifier-head compound but misses \"deficiency in G6PD,\" which uses a head-first, post-modified prepositional structure instead — pointing to syntax, not meaning, as the key differentiator.\n\nThe consistent pattern seems to be that \"deficiency [of/in] [enzyme]\" constructions, where a state noun takes a protein-naming prepositional complement, get treated by the model as biochemical descriptions rather than disease mentions, unlike fixed \"[Gene] deficiency\" compound names. This matches other cases like \"deficiency of norrin\" being labeled O in gold rather than as a disease entity.\n\nMaybe the distinction is that the PKU deficiency is presented as the defining cause of a named disease, while the norrin deficiency is a mechanistic intermediate step toward Coats telangiectasis rather than the disease itself — though this could also just be gold-standard inconsistency. Given I need exactly one \"Pattern Insight,\" I should pick the clearer, more defensible formulation of this distinction.\n\nThe fever case in the G6PD document likely is an outlier or annotation error, since symptoms like ichthyosis and spasticity are consistently left unlabeled elsewhere in similar contexts — I'll note this as a residual exception rather than stretching the pattern to