is a fixed nominal name (premodifier + head) or a free-standing, head-first construction whose complement is a protein/enzyme.\n\n**Isolate Trigger**\n\nThe model reads \"deficiency/deficient activity of/in [gene product]\" as a biochemical finding or mechanism rather than a disease name. The enzyme complement looks like a protein entity outside the schema, so the model returns O for the whole span. The gold span covers the entire phrase, including the enzyme name, so the model's miss is both a label and a boundary error.\n\n**Internal evidence from the same documents**\n\n- **Same head, different syntax (doc 1303173):** \"G6PD deficiency\" [772–787] is annotated SpecificDisease and is not a discrepancy. \"deficiency in G6PD A-\" is missed. The head noun is identical; the syntax differs.\n- **Same construction, gold O (doc 10484772):** \"results in a **deficiency of norrin**\" is not annotated. This is a true negative for the same surface construction, so \"deficiency of X\" alone cannot trigger SpecificDisease.\n- **Disease-defining frame (doc 10577908):** The deficiency phrase sits inside the \"characterized by ... \" list that defines SLS. In that same list, \"ichthyosis\" and \"spasticity\" are O, so the gold does not treat every list item as a disease.\n\n**Hypothesis Check**\n\n- The deficiency-nominal trigger explains three of the four discrepancies.\n- **Fever (doc 6859721)** is a residual outlier. It is a sign, not a deficiency construction, and the model's O is consistent with the gold convention in the same schema, where \"ichthyosis\" and \"spasticity\" are O. The SpecificDisease label on \"fever\" looks like gold annotation noise and should not be folded into this pattern.\n- The norrin true negative shows the rule needs a context condition. The construction is not a disease trigger by itself.\n\n## Step 2: Pattern Formulation\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Enzyme-Deficiency State Nominals Read as Biochemical Findings\n- **Confusion Trigger:** The head noun \"deficiency\" or \"deficient activity\" is followed by a prepositional complement (*of/in*) naming an enzyme or protein. The model treats the construction as a measurable molecular state, so it labels the span O and drops the enzyme name from the span. Free-standing, head-first syntax does not match the fixed compound disease-name pattern the model has learned.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** True positives use fixed, lexicalized disease names with a disease-category head: \"Coats disease,\" \"fragile X syndrome,\" \"ovarian cancer,\" and the compound \"G6PD deficiency.\" In the G6PD document, \"G6PD deficiency\" is correctly tagged while \"deficiency in G6PD A-\" is missed. Those true positives do not depend on a PP-complement construction, and the model handles them correctly.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):**\n  1. If the span is a fixed compound of the form [gene/enzyme] + \"deficiency\" (e.g., \"G6PD deficiency\"), label SpecificDisease.\n  2. If the head is \"deficiency\" or \"deficient activity\" followed by *of/in* + [enzyme/protein], label the full phrase, including the enzyme name, as SpecificDisease only when the sentence frames the deficiency as the defining condition of a named disease or phenotype (e.g., \"disease caused by a deficiency of…,\" \"characterized by… deficient activity of…,\" \"enzyme deficiency in G6PD A-\" as the phenotype).\n  3. If the deficiency is a downstream mechanistic intermediate with no disease-defining frame (e.g., \"results in a deficiency of norrin\"), keep O and label the protein as O.\n  4. Symptoms and signs (fever, ichthyosis, spasticity) are not triggered by this rule and remain O. The fever label should be audited as possible annotation noise.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_c